# --- Python / backend ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.env
.env.*
*.env.local
!.env.example
!*.env.example
# Deliberately committed: holds one PUBLIC https endpoint (VITE_API_BASE) and says so in its own
# header. Vite bakes it into the production bundle, so it is public by definition. Listed explicitly
# so the exception is a decision, not a side effect of it predating the rule above.
!frontend/.env.production

# --- Local secrets (never committed): prod connection strings etc. ---
.secrets/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# --- Node / frontend ---
node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
.vite/
*.local

# --- Capacitor native projects (generated; added in M7) ---
# iOS: the whole tree is ignored and its source files were force-added (`git add -f`). Left as-is
# because the App Store pipeline works and churn there is risk for no gain — but note the trap: a
# NEW native file is silently untracked until someone force-adds it too.
frontend/ios/

# Android deliberately does NOT use that pattern. The project carries real, hand-edited
# configuration — POST_NOTIFICATIONS in the manifest, targetSdk 35, the release signing block — and
# losing any of it to a silent ignore would produce a build that fails only at Play upload. So the
# sources track automatically and only genuinely GENERATED or SECRET paths are excluded:
frontend/android/.gradle/
frontend/android/build/
frontend/android/app/build/
frontend/android/local.properties
# Regenerated by `npx cap sync android` on every build:
frontend/android/app/src/main/assets/public/
frontend/android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.config.json
frontend/android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.plugins.json
frontend/android/app/capacitor.build.gradle
frontend/android/capacitor-cordova-android-plugins/
# Secrets — CI writes these from its own variable groups. NEVER commit them.
# Path-anchored rules only guard the directory they name: `frontend/android/*.jks` did NOT cover
# frontend/android/app/keystore.jks, which is exactly where Gradle puts one. Signing material is
# matched by EXTENSION, anywhere in the tree, so a new build layout can't quietly open the hole.
frontend/android/app/google-services.json
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/key.properties

# Apple signing + push keys (the .p8 is the APNs auth key; .p12/.mobileprovision are distribution).
**/*.p8
**/*.p12
**/*.mobileprovision

# --- Editors / OS ---
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Logs ---
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# --- Playwright MCP scratch ---
.playwright-mcp/

# --- Superpowers brainstorm mockups (local visual-companion scratch) ---
.superpowers/

# --- Throwaway verification screenshots (repo root only; real image assets live in frontend/public) ---
/*.png

# --- TypeScript incremental build cache ---
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- Local dev scratch (debug artifacts + one-off design concept mockups; never served,
#     not part of the app). Listed explicitly so a real frontend/public asset is never ignored. ---
/debug/
/frontend/public/phase3-growth-mockup.html
/frontend/public/progress-concepts.html
/frontend/public/skilltree-concepts.html
/frontend/public/skilltree-v2.html
/frontend/public/campaign-concept-a.html
/frontend/public/campaign-concept-b.html
/frontend/public/campaign-concept-c.html
/frontend/public/campaign-concept-v2.html
# ...and the art those concept pages load (mockup-only; the shipped copies live in src/assets).
/frontend/public/mockup-assets/

# --- Translation review output (regenerable) ---
# Only the generated REVIEW files. The translate_export directory itself stays tracked: the
# per-category source documents and the assistant replies imported from them are the audit trail for
# what was translated and from what, so they must not be ignored. A review file is derived state —
# `python -m app.jobs.run review-flagged --locale <loc>` rebuilds it from the DB at any time.
#
# NB the leading `backend/`: a pattern containing a slash is anchored to THIS file's directory, so a
# bare `content/translate_export/*-flagged-review.md` would look for a repo-ROOT content/ tree, which
# does not exist (the canonical tree is backend/content/). Anchored, as with every
# other path in this file.
/backend/content/translate_export/*-flagged-review.md

# Standalone design exploration — campaign concept pages (plain HTML, opened directly in a browser)
# plus a duplicated copy of the campaign art they reference. Nothing in src/ imports any of it, so it
# is working material rather than part of the app, and committing it would put ~3 MB of art in the
# repo a second time.
/frontend/design-mockups/

# Change-detection image QC pipeline (tools/change-pipeline). The TOOL is tracked — it is how more
# pairs get made — but its virtualenv and its generated output are not. `build/` holds derived
# JPEGs, heatmaps and manifests; the shipped copies live under frontend/public/assets/change and
# backend/content/change instead.
tools/change-pipeline/.venv/
tools/change-pipeline/build/
tools/change-pipeline/.pytest_cache/

# Fetched private content package (scripts/fetch_private_content.py). Never committed: it is the
# private corpus, and on a deploy it is produced by the build.
backend/.content/

# ── Local developer tooling under tools/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The SOURCE of these tools is worth keeping (tools/change-pipeline generates the change-detection
# manifests the app ingests). Their environments and outputs are not: change-pipeline's .venv alone
# is 183 MB, which is larger than every shipped asset in the repository combined.
tools/*/.venv/
tools/*/.pytest_cache/
tools/**/__pycache__/
# Pipeline working directories: intake photography, intermediate frames and generated manifests.
# Intake is unreleased source imagery and the output is private content — neither belongs in public
# source, and both are reproducible from the tool.
tools/*/intake/
tools/*/work/
tools/*/out/
tools/*/output/
